<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mataka Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1912 CE (approx.) · capital Mwembe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful Yao chiefdom, which grew rich on the ivory and slave trade between Lake Malawi and the Indian Ocean coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Yao chief Mataka I Nyambi built up a following on the highlands east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> in the early 19th century, gathering dependants and captives around his town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwembe">Mwembe</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niassa-province">Niassa</a> province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> visited Mwembe in 1866 and described a large, prosperous settlement.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The chiefdom lived from long-distance trade: caravans carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and enslaved people to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> ports and brought back cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and guns. Successive chiefs took the title Mataka. Coastal contact brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, which later Matakas adopted along with Swahili dress and literacy, making the Yao one of the most Muslim peoples of the interior.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mataka's people resisted the Portuguese <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niassa-company">Niassa Company</a> for two decades until its forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwembe">Mwembe</a> in 1912. Mataka's domain then became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>. Yao communities remain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, still largely Muslim.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1800 CE – 1912 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.52%;width:2.23%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1912 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mwembe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mataka I Nyambi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chiyao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mataka, Yao of Mataka</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=mataka-chiefdom" aria-label="Mataka Chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5491 2940 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mataka Chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 3196h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 3196h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mwembe. <a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=mataka-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

